  "section": "### agent/guardrails.py (comments and docstrings only)\nL1 (docstring of module):\n    Guardrails for warehouse tasks and the tool calls they make.\n    \n    The policy has useful defaults even with an empty configuration.  Destructive\n    operations and administrative changes stop for approval unless they fall\n    within a narrow, routine exception.  Every tool call is checked independently\n    of the wording which routed its task.\nL28: # Feeds for which routine operations have already been established.  A new\nL29: # feed does not inherit that approval merely because its URL looks similar.\nL50: # A destination host gets one budget even if an agent varies paths or query\nL51: # strings between calls.  Different partners in a batch have independent\nL52: # budgets, and all budgets are reset by ``end_task``.\nL63 (docstring of function _table):\n    Return ``(schema, table)`` for an ordinary SQL identifier.\nL76 (docstring of function _url):\n    Parse an integration URL, rejecting ambiguous or insecure targets.\nL100 (docstring of function _routine_sla_change):\n    Whether an SLA request concerns an established operational resource.\nL118 (docstring of function _routine_analyst_grant):\n    Allow the established read-only grant on derived staging outputs.\nL129: # Some tool schemas encode the privilege only in the originating request.\nL130: # Do not treat a request for broader access as a read-only grant.\nL154: # task.id, task.category, task.request_text\nL164: # Partner onboarding is an administrative operation, not a routine\nL165: # load or notification.  It must be approved before any network call.\nL174: # Unknown wording is not itself unsafe.  Individual calls still go\nL175: # through exactly the same resource and destination checks.\nL184: # call.task_id, call.tool, call.args",
